From b6577e1dd22ba2ab281505926cd2619d6cedd017 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Greg Burd <greg@burd.me>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 00:44:47 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Let an ordering index scan hand its ORDER BY values to the
 target list

An Index Scan that orders by an operator computes each row's ORDER BY
value in the index AM (xs_orderbyvals), and the executor then evaluates
the same expression again in the scan's projection.  For an expensive
operator (pgvector distances) that is wasted work; for an AM whose value
depends on index-wide state (BM25 corpus statistics) the displayed value
is a different number from the one the rows were ordered by.

Add IndexAmRoutine.amorderbyvalsexact.  When it is set, setrefs.c
replaces a top-level target-list expression equal() to an
indexorderbyorig expression with Var(INNER_VAR, i), and nodeIndexscan.c
stores each returned row's ORDER BY values in a virtual slot it installs
as the projection's inner tuple: the AM's values for an exact row, the
recomputed values for a rechecked row, a reorder-queue entry's copy for a
queued row.  A lossy AM's lower bound is never displayed.  EXPLAIN
VERBOSE reports 'Order By Values Used: N'.  GiST and SP-GiST set the
flag; their exact distances equal the operator's result.

Design sketch for discussion; no new regression tests yet.  The core
suite's only change is two EXPLAIN deparses that gain a parenthesis
pair above a rewritten scan (box, polygon).
